import Std
import Std.Tactic.BVDecide

/-!
Erdős Problem 302 — direct finite upper bound at n = 736.

This file encodes a subset of {1,...,736} as a 736-bit vector. Bit i represents
membership of i+1. Every forbidden reciprocal triple is generated externally
from the exact integer relation a*(b+c)=b*c and then embedded here as a Boolean
clause saying that not all three vertices may be present.

The theorem upper736_bits is intentionally independent of the MIP runtime. Lean's
bv_decide bit-blasts the Boolean/BitVec proposition, calls its SAT backend, and
checks the generated LRAT UNSAT certificate before producing the theorem.

No MIP result is trusted by this proof.
-/

namespace Erdos302F736BV

set_option maxRecDepth 1000000
set_option maxHeartbeats 0

def tripleOK (x : BitVec 736) (a b c : Nat) : Bool :=
  !(x.getLsbD (a-1) && x.getLsbD (b-1) && x.getLsbD (c-1))

